## 1. Introduction
As a carbon-zero energy carrier, hydrogen energy can effectively replace fossil energy and reduce environmental problems, and thus is considered to be the most promising energy source [1]. Electrocatalytic water splitting, which includes hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [2,3], is an effective way to obtain hydrogen energy. Compared with HER, OER has sluggish kinetics due to the four-electron transfer process, so it is very urgent to prepare highly active OER catalysts [4,5]. Noble metal-based catalysts such as Ru-based and Ir-based catalysts have outstanding OER performance [6,7], but the scarcity and high-cost limit their widespread applications. Fortunately, NiFe-based catalysts with abundant raw materials on earth have high OER activity and stability in alkaline solutions, making them a very cost-effective OER catalyst [8–10].
The electrocatalytic performance of NiFe-based catalysts depends on the electronic structure of the reactive sites and charge transfer rate [11]. Many studies have shown that Fe-doped $\gamma\cdot$ - NiOOH $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH) phase is a highly reactive site for OER [10,12–14]. Trotochaud et al. proposed that Fe doping can cause partial charge transfer from Ni to Fe, and Li et al. reported that $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ could promote the formation of $\mathsf{N i}^{4+}$ , which leads to highly catalytic activity [10,13]. Using operando UV–vis spectroscopy, Rao et al. attributed the increase of oxidized species in the OER to the formation of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{4+}$ species, and the Fe-doped samples have the highest intrinsic turnover frequency (number of $0_{2}$ evolved per oxidized state per second) [12]. It is worth noting that the formation of high valence metal ions in NiFe oxyhydroxide is influenced by the second-coordination-sphere metal ions [15]. In $\pi$ -symmetry d-orbitals $(\mathrm{t}_{2\mathrm{g}}),\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions have more electrons relative to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions, so under the bridging of bridging oxygen with $\pi$ -symmetry lone pairs, part of the electrons transfer from Ni ion to Fe ion to form high-valence metal ions [16]. However, how to rationally adjust the electronic structure of the NiFe-based catalysts to enhance the electron transfer from Ni to Fe to promote the generation of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{4+}$ species remains a challenge.
Recent studies have shown that the nature of the anionic ligands seriously affects the electron transfer efficiency from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [17,18]. Li et al. adsorbed carboxylate ligands (CLs) on the surface of NiFe hydroxide, and the results show that coordinated oxygen from ligand facilitates the charge transfer from Ni to Fe [17]. Similarly, He et al. proposed that benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (PTA) has a $\pi$ -conjugated benzene ring, which can interact with p orbitals of O atoms via $\pi$ -donation, leading to better electron delocalization and charge transfer between $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [18]. In addition, Jiang et al. found that $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ occupancy of metal affects the electron transfer between metal and bridging oxygen, such as vanadium ions with lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ occupancy, leading to a stronger partial electron transfer from $\pi$ -symmetric lone pair electrons of the bridging $0^{2-}$ to vanadium ions [16]. As we all know, high-spin Fe ions have lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ occupancy than low-spin Fe ions [19], and 2,5-dioxidobenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate as a kind of conjugated CLs, used to prepare Fe-based catalysts with high spin Fe, which suggests that it has strong electron transfer from bridging oxygen to Fe [20]. However, few studies have reported differences in the interaction of conjugated and non-conjugated CLs with metal centers, which can affect the intermetallic electron transfer and electronic structure of catalyst. Inspired by the above studies, we thus want to know how conjugated and non-conjugated CLs affect the oxidation state and spin-related electron transfer of metal ions, which is very important for understanding the electronic structure of catalysts and rational design of electrocatalysts with efficient OER. Besides, catalysts with $\pi$ orbitals have recently attracted extensive attention from researchers because $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking not only ensures the stability of the catalysts but also accelerates the electron migration from the bulk phase to the surface within the catalysts.[21,22] Although the electronic structure and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking on electrocatalysts have been extensively studied, the mechanism of how $\pi$ -interaction affects the electronic structure and electron transfer of electrocatalysts to enhance OER activity is still unclear.
Herein, we designed and synthesized a series of NiFe-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) composed of conjugated or non-conjugated carboxylate ligands (CLs) with the following considerations: (1) two types of NiFe-based MOFs are formed from conjugated or unconjugated ligands, respectively, which is beneficial to realize the influence of $\pi$ -interaction on the performance of bimetallic MOF-based electrocatalysts; (2) under the different ligand fields, the oxidation state and the local spin configuration of the metal centers can be modulated, which is helpful to explore the effect of $\pi$ -interaction on the electronic structure of the reaction active sites; and (3) the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of conjugated CLs accelerates the electron migration within the catalyst, which meets the rapid generation of OER active species. Given the above considerations, taking biphenyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (BPDC), PTA, and 1,4-cyclohexane dicarboxylic acid (CHDA) as conjugated and non-conjugated CLs, we prepared NiFe-BPDC MOFs, NiFe-PTA MOFs, and NiFe-CHDA MOFs on nickel foam (NF). The turnover frequency (TOF) of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF was significantly increased compared to that of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF, indicating that pinteraction enhances the intrinsic activity of the catalyst. Under the conjugated CLs field, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generate $\gamma{-}\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ - $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ which are highly active reaction sites with high-valent Ni modified by high-spin density Fe proved by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Rietveld refinement, density functional theory (DFT) calculations, and in-situ Raman spectra. With larger pinteraction energy, NiFe-BPDC generates OER active species rapidly. So, we propose that the $\pi$ -interaction optimizes the electronic structure of the reaction active sites and accelerates the electron transfer within the catalyst to promote the rapid generation of highly OER active sites. Furthermore, we discussed the OER mechanism through the reaction intermediates and products. NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF can rapidly complete the deprotonation and deoxygenation steps, and it requires only $288~\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential to reach $100\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ with $100\mathrm{h}$ of stability, which means promising industrial application.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals
Nickel foam (NF), nickel(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $98\%$ , N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -Dimethylformamide $(C_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{NO}$ , DMF), iron(III) nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $98.5\%$ , biphenyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{{'}C_{14}H_{10}O_{4}}$ , $B P D C,97\%$ ), benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{6}0_{4}$ , PTA, $99\%$ ), trans-1,4-cyclohexane dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\C_{8}H_{12}O_{4}}$ , CHDA, $97\%$ , ruthenium(IV) oxide ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ , $99.9\%$ , sulfuric acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}S O_{4}$ , $98\%$ , and ethanol $\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}0\right)$ were purchased from Macklin reagent Co., Ltd. Note that before the preparation, the purchased NF was carefully treated with sonicated in $1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}0_{4}$ ethanol, and deionized water for $15\mathrm{min}$ in turn to remove the surface impurities such as the surface oxide layer formed by exposing the NF in air and possible organic and inorganic contaminants on the surface of NF.
## 2.2. Electrode fabrication
## 2.2.1. Preparation of $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF
Typically, 4 mmol ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ and 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $45~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. Then $6~\mathrm{mL}$ pure water containing
0.9 mmol NaOH was added dropwise. After stirring at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , a piece of pretreated NF $(1.5\times3.8~\mathrm{cm})$ ) was immersed into the homogeneous solution and heated in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $6.5\mathrm{~h~}$ After cooling to room temperature, the NF were washed with deionized water and ethanol three times, then dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ to get $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF
Typically, 4 mmol $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $25\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, while 3 mmol BPDC was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF under ultrasonication for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Then $6~\mathrm{mL}$ pure water containing 0.9 mmol NaOH was added dropwise. After stirring at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , a piece of pretreated NF (1. $5\ \times\ 3.8\ \mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ ) was immersed into the homogeneous solution and heated in a $100~\mathrm{\mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $6.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the NF were washed with deionized water and ethanol three times, then dried at $80^{\circ}C$ to get NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF.
## 2.2.3. Preparation of NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF
The preparation of NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF was like that of NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF, except using PTA instead of BPDC.
## 2.2.4. Preparation of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF
The preparation of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF was like that of NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF, except using CHDA instead of BPDC.
## 2.2.5. Preparation of $R u O_{2}/N F$
$20\mathrm{\mg\RuO_{2}}$ was dispersed into $180~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ pure water, $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ Nafion, and $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ isopropanol under ultrasonication for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of homogeneous ink was dropped onto NF. After that, it dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to get $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ .
## 2.2.6. Preparation of BPDC, PTA, or CHDA induced conversion of cNiFeOOH/NF
Typically, 3 mmol BPDC was dissolved in $45~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF under ultrasonication for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then a piece of NF $1.5\times3.8~\mathrm{cm}^{\cdot}$ supported $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH was immersed into the homogeneous solution and heated in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $6.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the NF were washed with deionized water and ethanol three times, then dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ to get $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-BPDC/NF. The preparation of $\gamma-$ NiFeOOH-PTA/NF and $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-CHDA/NF was like that of $\gamma\mathrm{.}$ - NiFeOOH-BPDC/NF, except using PTA and CHDA instead of BPDC.
## 2.3. Materials characterizations
Raman microscope under an excitation of $532~\mathrm{nm}$ laser light with the power of $50\mathrm{mW}$ .
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
The CHI 660E electrochemical workstation with a threeelectrode system was used to test the electrochemical properties of the materials at room temperature. The graphite rod was used as the counter electrode, and the calibrated $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode as the reference electrode. The NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF as a working electrode was controlled to have an area of $1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ immersed in 1 M KOH electrolyte. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) polarization curves were measured with $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ and $95\%$ IR corrected, and all the measured potentials were referred to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) via the Nernst equation $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{E_{Hg/Hgo}+0.0592\times\ p H+E_{H g/H g O}^{\theta}})$ . See Supporting Information for details about the Tafel, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), turnover frequency (TOF), and the OER Faradic efficiency (FE).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structural characteristics of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF
We used two methods to prepare NiFe (hydro)oxides catalysts with electron-rich carboxylic groups. One method is the directcoordination method, which coordinates metal ions with different CLs to form MOFs (Fig. 1a), and the other method is the precursorcoordination method, which coordinates the $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH precursor with different CLs (Fig. S1). The catalysts synthesized by the directcoordination method are NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF, NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF, and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF, while those synthesized by the precursor-coordination method are $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-BPDC/NF, cNiFeOOH-PTA/NF, and $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-CHDA/NF. As captured via FESEM, it shows that $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF is a stacked plate structure (Figs. 1b and S2a). The materials synthesized by the precursorcoordination method kept the stacked structure of $\gamma{\mathrm{-NiFeOOH/}}$ NF (Fig. S3a–f). However, the materials synthesized by the direct-coordination method have more flexible and tunable structures. NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF is stacked by larger, thicker, and rough plates (Figs. 1c and S2b), but NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF has a regular sheet arrangement (Figs. 1d and S2c). Especially, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is a porous structure made of smaller and thinner nanosheets with curled edges (Figs. 1e and S2d). This suggests that MOFs have variable structures that can be tuned by different ligands. Specifically, BPDC and PTA have both $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated and carboxylic groups, while CHDA only has carboxylic groups. Therefore, we divide the CLs into two types, one is conjugated CLs, including BPDC and PTA, and the other is non-conjugated CLs, including CHDA. On this basis, we propose that having pinteraction leads to flexible structures with curled edges and avoids the self-stacking of nanosheets, which exposes abundant reactive sites and facilitates oxygen desorption [23,24].
The PXRD patterns and FT-IR spectra further illustrate the successful synthesis of the materials. First, the PXRD patterns of $\gamma$ - NiFeOOH and MOFs are shown in Fig. 3a. Three peaks centered at $12.8^{\circ},25.9^{\circ}$ , and $37.1^{\circ}$ for $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH can be identified, corresponding well to (00 3), (0 06), and (101) facets of $\gamma$ -NiOOH [28], as well as $35.0^{\circ}$ and $60.9^{\circ}$ corresponding to $\gamma$ -FeOOH. The crystal phases of the MOFs with different organic linkers are completely different from $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH but are consistent with the simulated one from the reported Ni-MOFs [21,29], confirming that the main crystal phases of these MOFs are identical to the Ni-MOFs. And iron doping could be proved by elemental mapping (Fig. 2f) and ICPOES elemental analysis. It is worth mentioning that a peak with 2h value of 25.4 were observed, corresponding to typical $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking by H-type aggregates [30]. Generally, H-type aggregates are a face-to-face molecular arrangement with higher $\pi$ -electron conjugation [31]. As the length of the organic ligands increases, the first diffraction peak of MOFs shifts to lower angles, indicating larger lattice spacing. What’s more, NiFe-BPDC and NiFe-PTA belong to p-1 space group, while NiFe-CHDA MOFs belong to $C2/m$ space group. The space group affects the final morphology of the materials, in which NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is a sheet structure (Fig. 1e) but NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF is composed of large and thick plates stacked (Fig. 1c). Besides, the crystallinity of NiFe-BPDC MOFs is significantly enhanced compared to NiFe-CHDA MOFs, indicating that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has a more stable structure, which is attributed to a stronger conjugation [21]. Second, the PXRD peaks of the materials prepared by the precursor-coordination method are almost the same as those of the $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH precursor (Fig. S6a). The results show that the materials with carboxylate coordinated on the $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH surface did not change the dominant crystalline phase.
As shown in the FT-IR spectra (Fig. 3b), NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF, NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF, and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF all have strong carboxyl vibration peaks. The carboxyl group has two strong bands due to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching vibration around $1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and another strong band at $1230{-}1140\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the stretching of the $c{-}0$ bond [32]. Usually, the acid derivatives have $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ stretching vibrations in the region of $3450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [32]. More importantly, these three materials have characteristic peaks of carbon skeleton vibrations from different ligands, respectively. On the one hand, the $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ stretching vibrations were observed at 1410 and $1295~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in CHDA [32]. On the other hand, the broadband between 1400 and $1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ indicates the benzene group in BPDC and PTA [33]. Generally, peaks around $2900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ owing to $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching vibrations. The $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ in-plane ring bending vibrations are normally observed as several strong to weak intensity sharp bands in the region $1300{-}1000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the CAH out-of-plane bending vibrations appear in the region $950{-}780~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [32]. M O and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M-O-H}$ related vibrations normally observed at $470\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $540~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [34]. Peak in the region $2400{-}2300~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ owing to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Besides, the FT-IR spectra of the materials prepared by the precursorcoordination method showed that they have carboxylic groups (Fig. S6b), but the vibration of the carbon skeleton is not obvious. This further confirms that the precursor-coordination method does not exert the space-confined effect of carbon skeleton from different ligands, thereby explaining why only the direct-coordination method could generate NiFeO QDs.
In conclusion, compared with the precursor-coordination method, the direct-coordination method not only is more suitable and convenient for large-scale production but also yields MOFs with better crystallinity, which is helpful to utilize the carbon skeleton of different ligands further to adjust the morphology and structure of the material. Specifically, the ligand with $\pi$ - interaction results in materials with layered morphologies of curled edges, smaller QDs size edge reaction sites, and more stable structures, which endows NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF with the potential to be a highly active and robust OER catalyst.
## 3.2. Electrocatalytic OER performance of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF
We used $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF catalyst as a research model to explain the roles of $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated and carboxylic groups in promoting OER catalytic performance. First, in Fig. 4a, we evaluate the OER catalytic activity of $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH and MOFs-based materials. Compared with $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH, MOFs-based materials significantly enhanced the OER activity, of which NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF with pinteraction had more excellent OER activity. Specifically, NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF only required an overpotential of $288~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , such OER activity even exceeds that of commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalysts and recently reported MOFsbased OER catalysts (Figs. 4a and 5c). Notably, the current density of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF increases by an order of magnitude over the previously reported $\mathsf{A}_{2.7}\mathsf{B}\mathrm{-MOF-FeCo}_{1.6}$ as MOFs-based OER catalysts at the same overpotential (Table S3) [35]. Besides, the materials with carboxylate coordinated on the $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH surface could also enhance the OER activity. In Fig. S7a, the OER activities of $\gamma\cdot$ - NiFeOOH-BPDC/NF, $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-PTA/NF, and $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH-CHDA/ NF were higher than $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF. From the above information, we conclude that the carboxylic groups enhance the activity of OER, and $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated could further improve the activity.
We studied the reaction kinetics of OER in terms of EIS and Tafel slope. As shown in Fig. 4c and Table S4, for $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF, the Nyquist plot shows about a $50^{\circ}$ line and a limited conductivity, which is consistent with the semi-infinite diffusion behavior [36]. Compared with $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF $(495.30\ \Omega$ of $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\mathbf{,}$ ) and NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF $51.50\ \Omega$ of $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\mathbf{,}$ , NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has lower charge transfer resistance ( $5.15\ \Omega$ of $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\mathbf{,}$ . It is reported that the $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated and carboxylic groups synergistically improve OER kinetics, which is further demonstrated by the Tafel slope. Compared with $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF, the rest of the materials with carboxyl groups have smaller Tafel slopes. (Fig. 4b and Fig. S7b). However, when higher current density was applied, compared with NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF showed a smaller Tafel slope and faster reaction kinetics, indicating that $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated could promote the transfer rate of electrons and provide more electron channels, which is consistent with the OER activity in the polarization curve.
For OER catalysts, intrinsic activity enhancement is important. Therefore, we evaluated the intrinsic activity of the catalyst by the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA), TOF, ECSAnormalized current density, and mass-normalized current density (mass activity) at an applied overpotential of $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ (1.53 V). We calculated the value of electric double-layer capacitances $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ proportional to ECSA (Figs. 4d and S8) and then obtained the ECSA of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is almost twice that of NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF (Fig. 5b). This is consistent with the fact that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has smaller edge reactive sites, which could exert size effects, allowing more reactive sites to be exposed in the electrolyte. In addition, at an applied overpotential of $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , the TOF and mass activity of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF are 6.29 times and 13.38 times higher than those of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF (Fig. 5b and Table S5), respectively. And ECSAnormalized current density of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is higher than that of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF for a given voltage (Fig. 8h). This indicates that the intrinsic OER activity of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is higher than that of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF. All the above tests demonstrated that $\pi$ -interaction could enhance the OER intrinsic activity of the catalyst.
As shown in Fig. 5a, we calculated the FE by comparing the experimental gas yield with the theoretical gas yield, and the results show that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has a stable oxygen yield and $98.56\%$ Faradaic efficiency. More importantly, we evaluated the stability of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF at large current densities $(100\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). After operating the NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF at a current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 5d), the voltage increased by $2.27\%$ , which is almost negligible, indicating the good stability of the NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF. As an anode electrode with nearly $100\%$ Faradaic efficiency and stable operation at a high current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ for $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ , NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has good prospects for practical applications in electrocatalytic water splitting for hydrogen production.
## 3.3. Oxidation state and spin-related electron transfer of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF
NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF shows significantly better OER activity than NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF. To explore the mechanism, we analyzed the differences in the electronic structures of these catalysts in terms of their oxidation state and local spin configuration. The survey spectra of XPS are provided in Fig. S9a, and in Fig. 6a, for the initial $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF, the XPS peak of $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ is at $856.50~\mathrm{eV},$ and the XPS peak of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ is at 855.29 eV [37]. Notably, the peak of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF positively shift $0.29\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and $0.02\mathrm{eV}$ to the binding energy of $855.58\mathrm{eV}$ and 855.31 eV, suggesting that the partial electrons transfer from Ni to a neighboring O [17], and generated more $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ ions (Table S6). On the contrary, the peak of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ for NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF negatively shifts 0.41 eV to the binding energy of $854.88\:\mathrm{eV}$ , indicating partial electrons transfer from the neighboring O to Ni. The presence of ${\sf N i}^{0}$ may be the reason for the nickel substrate or the electronic interaction between bridging $0^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , but these do not influence the fact that the negative shift of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ binding energy in NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF. These implied Ni ions are electronaccepting sites in NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF but electron-donating sites in NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF. Furthermore, we speculate that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF form electron transport channels from Ni to Fe via bridging $0^{2-}$ , while NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF does not. In addition, the result of XPS spectra of Fe is consistent with our hypothesis. As shown in Fig. 6b, for the initial $\gamma$ -NiFeOOH/NF, the XPS peak of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ is at $712.30\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ is at $706.50\mathrm{eV}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ is at $709.86\mathrm{eV}$ [38]. What’s more, the peak of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF negatively shift $0.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $0.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ to the binding energy of $709.36\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $709.45\ \mathrm{eV}$ , while it for NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF positively shift $0.53\ \mathrm{eV}$ to the binding energy of $710.39\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . These observations suggest partial electron transfer from Ni to Fe in NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF through oxygen bridges $(0^{2-})$ between metal ions. It is further confirmed by DFT calculations. We obtained the refined structure of NiFe-BPDC MOFs and NiFe-CHDA MOFs by Rietveld refinement of the computational models with PXRD data (Fig. 7a and Fig. S10a). Geometry optimization and energy optimization of the structure were carried out to obtain the calculated Mulliken charges for metal sites and the partial density of states (PDOS) of metal ions in NiFe-BPDC MOFs and NiFe-CHDA MOFs. Mulliken charge analysis shows partial charges of 0.882 and $1.023~e$ on the Ni and Fe sites in NiFe-BPDC MOFs and 0.889 and $_{0.991~e}$ on the Ni and Fe sites in NiFe-CHDA MOFs (Table S7), indicating that more electron transfer from Ni to Fe in the NiFe-BPDC MOFs through oxygen bridges between metal ions. Although DFT calculations cannot determine the exact valence of each metal in the multimetal materials, the trend of partial charges on Ni and Fe sites obtained from Mulliken charge analysis is consistent with the result of XPS [16]. The partial charge transfer from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ also induces a downshift of the Ni d-band to penetrate the p-band of oxygen intermediates, thus optimizing the binding strength of intermediates (Fig. 9d) [17].
It is still unclear why electrons transfer from Ni to Fe, which is related to the local spin configuration of metals. We investigated the local spin configurations of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ as they are reagents in the preparation of catalysts. As shown in Fig. 6c, the psymmetry $\left(\mathbf{t}_{2\tt g}\right)$ d-orbitals of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in the low-spin state are fully occupied due to its ${\mathsf{d}}^{8}$ configuration, leading to electron–electron $(e^{-},~e^{-})$ repulsion as the dominating role between the bridging $0^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (Fig. 6d) [17,39]. But for $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ with ${\mathsf{d}}^{5}$ configuration, its unpaired electrons in the $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ d-orbitals lead to partial electron transfer from $\pi$ -symmetry lone pairs of the bridging $0^{2-}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ via $\pi$ -donation (Fig. 6d) [16,40]. Thus, when bridging $0^{2-}$ bridges $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (Fig. 6d), the electron transfer from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ can be strengthened via $\pi$ -donation from the bridging $0^{2-}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . More importantly, the stronger $\pi$ -donation between Fe and O, the better it promotes the electron transfer from $\mathsf{N i}$ to Fe. It is well known that $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions in high spin states have lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ occupancy than those in low spin states (Fig. 6c), so it has stronger $\pi$ -donation from the bridging $0^{2-}$ . Therefore, $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ in a high spin state could promote more electron transfer from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , resulting in NiO-Fe electron transport channels and more high valence Ni atoms.
Generally, whether the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ is in a high spin state or a low spin state depends on the relative magnitude of the crystal-field splitting energy and the spin-pairing energy. In the octahedral crystal field, since the d orbitals $(\mathbf{d}_{z2},\mathbf{d}_{\mathbf{x}2\mathbf{-y}2},\mathbf{d}_{\mathbf{xy}},\mathbf{d}_{\mathbf{x}z},\mathbf{d}_{\mathbf{y}z})$ of metal ions have different extension directions and shapes, their interactions with ligands are different, leading to different energy changes. According to the change of the d orbital energy for the metal ion, it is divided into two sets of degenerate orbitals ( $\mathrm{\dot{\t}}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ and $\mathbf{e_{g}},$ , and the energy difference between these two sets of degenerate orbitals is called the crystal-field splitting energy. As shown in Fig. 6c, the d orbital of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ has a ${\mathsf{d}}^{8}$ configuration, so there is only one low spin state. However, the d orbital of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ has a ${\mathsf{d}}^{5}$ configuration, possessing multiple electron configurations [40]. When three electrons of the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ fill the $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ orbital, whether the rest of the electrons fill the $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ orbital or the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e_{g}}}$ orbital requires consideration of the relative values of the crystal-field splitting energy and the spinpairing energy. Crystal-field splitting energy is related to the intrinsic properties of the ligand and the oxidation state of the metal ion. As the oxidation state of the metal ion increases, it proves to interact more strongly with the ligand, leading to a crystal field splitting energy larger than the spin pairing energy and the formation of metal ions with low spin states. First, in Fig. 6b, the XPS peak of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ for NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF positively shifts $0.53{\mathrm{~eV}},$ , implying that Fe ions have higher oxidation states and interact more strongly with CHDA, leading to the easier formation of Fe ions in low spin states. For NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF, the opposite is true, so it tends to form high-spin states of Fe. Second, compared with NiFe-CHDA MOFs, the spin density (the intensity of spin-up minus spin-down) of Fe 3d orbitals for NiFe-BPDC MOFs is significantly increased (Fig. 7b), indicating a high spin state [19]. Third, the wider Fe 3d orbital width of NiFe-CHDA MOFs indicates that Fe ions have stronger interactions with CHDA and are prone to the low-spin states, but the narrower Fe 3d orbital width of NiFeBPDC MOFs indicates that Fe ions have weaker interactions with BPDC and are prone to high-spin states. In addition, the variation in the packing of the layers also affects the spin state of metal ions [41]. Due to the coplanarity of the benzene ring, $\pi$ -interaction allows NiFe-BPDC MOFs to exist in a stable lamellar structure, as demonstrated by SEM images (Fig. 1e), which may be one of the reasons for the Fe ion in a high spin state. The increased spin density of Fe ions in NiFe-BPDC MOFs proved by DFT calculations, facilitates the electron transfer from Ni to Fe through forming lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ orbital occupancy and enhancing $\pi$ -donation between $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and $0^{2-}$ .
Based on the above discussions, we propose that conjugated and non-conjugated CLs have different interaction mechanisms with metal centers leading to different oxidation states and local spin configurations of metal ions in NiFe-BPDC MOFs and NiFeCHDA MOFs. Under the ligand field, the Fe ions in NiFe-BPDC MOFs with a stable layered stacking structure have a lower oxidation state and weaker interaction with the ligand compared to NiFeCHDA MOFs. These promote $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions in NiFe-BPDC MOFs to be in higher spin states and lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ orbital occupancy, which results in a stronger $\pi$ -donation between $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and bridging $0^{2-}$ to facilitate rapid electron transfer from Ni to Fe to form more high-valence Ni ions. In addition, more delocalized electrons in NiFe-BPDC $\mathsf{M O F s}/$ NF also promote the electron transfer between Ni and Fe. This regulation of the oxidation state and local spin configuration in the metal center by the conjugation CLs leads to the Ni-O-Fe electron channels, which guide the rational design of the electronic structures for excellent OER sites.
## 3.4. Mechanism for improving electrocatalytic activity of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF
To identify the actual OER active site of the catalysts on the NF electrode, we carried out in-situ and operando Raman spectra by using a customized electrochemical cell (Fig. 8a). As shown in the Raman spectra (Fig. 8b-e), with increasing the applied voltage or residence time of the catalyst in the electrolyte, as a general trend, a pair of bands at 474 and $565~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared on the asprepared catalysts. The band of $474~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to Ni-O vibrations in $\gamma$ -NiOOH [27,42], and the band of $565~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is Fe (IV)–OH bond [43]. However, the relative intensities of the two bands of the catalysts are different. For NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF, the ratio of the intensity at the $565~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band to that at the $474~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band is much higher than that for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF, indicating that more FeOOH is generated in quantity comparable to that of $\gamma$ -NiOOH. Because NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has Ni-O-Fe electron channels but NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF does not, we presume that the actual OER active sites of NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF are $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ which has an excellent electronic structure of highly active Ni sites modified by high spin Fe, while those of NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF are $\gamma$ -NiOOH and FeOOH which are just two common different active sites. Recent studies have shown that when the Fe content is less than $25\%$ , the active site of the catalyst could be considered as a partial replacement of Ni atoms by Fe atoms and is described as $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ [44]. However, when the Fe content is more than $25\%$ , there will be more FeOOH generated, and the active site of the catalyst could be described as NiOOH and FeOOH. As shown by ICP-OES measurements, the atomic ratio of Fe content in the NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF bulk phases are consistent. But on the surface of the catalysts, the atomic ratio of the NiFe-BPDC $\mathsf{M O F s}/$ NF and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF integrated from the XPS are different. We inferred the active reaction sites on the surface of the catalyst during the OER process by surface-sensitive characterization techniques, such as operando Raman spectra and XPS. As shown in Table S9, the Fe content on the surface of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is $20.59\%$ , which is less than $25\%$ and tends to generate $\gamma{-}\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ - $\phantom{+}_{\mathrm{{x}}}\mathrm{{Fe}}_{\mathrm{{x}}}00\mathrm{{H}}$ active sites on the surface of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF (Fig. 8b and c). On the contrary, the Fe content on the surface of NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF is $36.33\%$ , which is more than $25\%$ and tends to generate $\gamma$ -NiOOH and FeOOH active sites on the surface of NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF (Fig. 8e). This is consistent with the stronger intensity of the peak at $565~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF (Fig. 8e), and in line with our assumption that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generated Ni-O-Fe electron channel while NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF did not (Fig. 6d). As shown in Fig. 8b, when the applied potential is above $1.3{\mathrm{V}}$ , NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generates $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ electrochemically active sites. At this time, the voltage was reduced to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ again, and the peak value of $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ does not change, so the phase change is irreversible. These indicate that $\gamma{-}\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ - $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{{x}}}\mathsf{F e}_{\mathrm{{x}}}00\mathsf{H}$ is the main OER active site for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and can exist stably in alkaline electrolytes. Electrochemical and structural studies have shown that high OER activity requires a large amount of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{4+}$ and a more active anodic oxidation $\gamma$ -NiOOH structure [10]. As shown in Figs. 6d and 8b, the Ni-O-Fe electron channel formed by NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF could provide more high-valence Ni atoms and more active $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{OOH}$ structure, indicating the excellent OER activity, which is in good agreement with the performance study results (Fig. 4a).
As shown in the Raman spectra, the peaks at 1287 and $1610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the bpdc2- species (Fig. 8b and c) [27,45], the peak at 1425 and $1610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching from the benzene ring in PTA ligands (Fig. 8d) [32], while the peak at $1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ascribable to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching from the carboxylate in the CHDA ligands (Fig. 8e) [46], which corresponds to the information obtained from the FTIR spectra (Fig. 3b). And a peak at $1064~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the superoxo species $(00^{-})$ was observed for the NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF (Fig. 8e) [47]. While the catalyst generates active reaction sites for OER, we observe an interesting phenomenon that the peaks at $1610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ or $1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ associated with carbon species decreased gradually, implying the desorption of carbon species is a prerequisite to oxyhydroxide formation [27]. However, it is difficult to distinguish whether it is mainly the $\pi$ -interaction or the carboxylic groups that promote the formation of OER sites. Therefore, we further compared the time to generate OER active sites for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF with NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF. Specifically, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generated $\gamma{-}\mathsf{N i}_{1}$ $\phantom{+}\mathbf{\phantom{x}}\mathbf{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathsf{H}$ active sites in only $60~s$ at an applied voltage of $1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 8c), while NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF generated $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ active sites in $280\ s$ at an applied voltage of $1.4\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. 8d). But for NiFeCHDA MOFs/NF, it took almost 9 times longer to form $\gamma$ -NiOOH and FeOOH active sites than NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF at a higher applied voltage of $1.5{\mathrm{~V}}$ (Fig. 8e). Among them, the first two catalysts have both $\pi$ -interaction and carboxylic groups, while the latter catalyst only has carboxylic groups. Although it has been recently shown that carboxyl groups as Lewis bases can accelerate proton transfer and promote the adsorption, activation, and dissociation of hydroxyl ions $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ , thus promoting sluggish OER kinetics [17]. In this paper, the carboxyl group is one of the important reasons for the superior OER activity of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF, NiFe-PTA MOFs/NF, and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF compared with cNiFeOOH/NF. However, for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF and NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF, which both have carboxyl groups, the rate of active site formation and OER performance are very different. Fig. 8f compares the $\pi$ -interaction energy, time to generate OER active sites, and overpotential at $100~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for NiFe-BPDC MOFs and NiFe-CHDA MOFs. Specifically, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF has larger $\pi$ - interaction energy, shorter time to generation reaction active sites, and lower overpotential at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ $(0.8559~\mathrm{eV},~80~\$ s and $288~\mathrm{mV}$ ), while NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NF has almost no $\pi$ -interaction energy, longer time to generate active reaction sites, and higher overpotential at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ (0.0513 eV, 600 s, and $361~\mathrm{{\mV}}$ ).
Therefore, we cannot ignore the important role of $\pi$ -interaction in excellent OER, which can provide the catalyst with electron transport channels from the bulk to the surface to facilitate rapid electron transfer within the catalyst and produce more efficient OER sites faster at a lower voltage. We further discuss the reasons for $\pi$ -interaction to enhance the OER activity of the catalyst as follows.
Together with the previous discussion on the oxidation states and local spin configurations of metal ions, we propose that the $\pi$ -interaction forms two electron transport channels that accelerate electron transport parallel to and between layers within NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF, thereby facilitating the rapid generation of high active species (Fig. 9a). On the one hand, the conjugation CLs modulate the oxidation state and the local spin configuration of the metal centers, leading to the creation of Ni-O-Fe electron transfer channel, which is electrons transfer parallel to the layer through-bond electron transport mechanism. Specifically, under the field of conjugation CLs, the Fe ions of NiFe-BPDC MOFs have weaker interaction with the ligand, promoting the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ of NiFeBPDC MOFs to be in higher spin states and lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ orbital occupancy, which results in the stronger $\pi$ -donation between $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and bridging $0^{2-}$ , facilitating electrons transfer from Ni to Fe parallel to the layer and forming $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1{-}\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ with highly active Ni sites. On the other hand, NiFe-BPDC MOFs are the H-type aggregate with high $\pi$ -electron conjugation proved by PXRD, and the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking provides a large number of delocalized electrons proved by XPS and UV–vis DRS, which allows electrons to overlap in space and orbitals, and thus electrons transfer between layers throughspace electron transport mechanism, accelerating the electron migration from the bulk phase to the surface [22]. Compared with NiFe-CHDA MOFs, NiFe-BPDC MOFs has larger $\pi$ -interaction energy, shorter reaction site generation time, and lower overpotential at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . Following the above discussion, $\pi$ -interaction plays an important role in the electronic structure and electron transfer rate, which facilitate the rapid generation of highly active sites and excellent OER activity in the catalyst.
To better understand the OER mechanism of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/ NF, we discussed the changes that occurred on the surface of NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF during the OER process through the reaction intermediates and products. As shown in the structural formula (Fig. 9a), when the surface of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF is in equilibrium with the alkaline electrolyte, undercoordinated surface O sites are saturated with H by forming OH bridges, and undercoordinated surface metal sites are saturated with OH [48]. During the OER process (Fig. 9c), as a general trend, the valence state of Ni in NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF increased rapidly from Ni(II) to Ni(III) with increasing applied voltage, allowing a rapid deprotonation step at the Ni reaction active sites (step 1 of the OER mechanism scheme in Fig. 9b), which due to the two electron transport channels in NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generated by the $\pi$ -interaction discussed earlier. After this, under the attack of voltage and alkaline electrolyte, Fe(IV)–OH bonds appeared, which indicated that both Ni and Fe were chemically oxidized in the reaction intermediates of OER [49]. It has been shown by operando Mössbauer spectroscopy that Fe cations in $\gamma$ -NiFe LDH could be in the $^{4+}$ oxidation state [15]. The high spin $\mathsf{F e}^{4+}$ with ${\mathsf{d}}^{4}$ configuration plays an important role in stabilizing the intermediate O radicals during the formation of Ni-OOH [50]. This also confirms that we obtained highly active Ni sites modified by high spin density Fe through the $\pi$ - interaction of the ligand.
The OER starts with Mars-Van Krevelen deprotonation, resulting in a gradual increase in the valence state of the metal ions (step 1 and step 2 in Fig. 9b), which has been the generally assumed mechanism [48]. After this, deoxygenation and in situ formation of oxygen vacancies $(0\upnu)$ lead to a decrease in the valence state of the metal ions (see step 3 and step 4 in Fig. 9b) [51]. To further investigate the structure after OER, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF after stability testing (after $100\mathrm{h}$ at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , in Fig. 5d) were analyzed by electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy and XPS. The EPR spectroscopy is very sensitive to the production and concentration of $0_{\mathrm{v}}$ [52]. As shown in Fig. 9e, the EPR spectrum of the NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF exhibits a larger peak at $\mathbf{g}=2.0025$ after the OER, which indicates the generation of $0_{\mathrm{v}}$ . Besides, the formation of $0_{\mathrm{v}}$ was also confirmed by XPS (Fig. 9f). The O 1s can be appropriately divided into four parts, which are lattice oxygen bonded to metals $(\mathrm{I},529.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ [53], defect sites with low oxygen coordination $\mathrm{~\textit~{~I~}~}531.2\mathrm{~eV})$ [54], $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ or hydroxyl groups in the crystal structure (III, 531.9 eV) [55,56], and $c{-}0$ or absorbed water on the surface $\mathrm{(I\nabla,533.2~eV}.$ ) [55,56]. As shown in Table S10, oxygen defect with low oxygen coordination and lattice oxygen of the NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF increased after the OER. When the oxygen atoms escape, the resulting $0_{\mathrm{v}}$ could produce extra electrons in the surrounding metal atoms, which lowers the valence state of the metal atoms [51]. As shown in Fig. 9c, with further increased potential, Ni(II)-O is observed. And after OER, the binding energy of Ni and Fe for NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF was negatively shifted (Fig. S12a-b). This indicates that both Ni and Fe ions are reduced to low-valence metal ions after the OER. It is worth noting that the changes here are incomplete phase changes, and the oxidation states of both Ni and Fe are measured as weighted averages of $^{2+}$ to $^{4+}$ [49]. In addition, as shown in the FT-IR spectrum of NiFeBPDC MOFs/NF after OER (Fig. S13a), the intensity of the $c{-}0$ bond peak decreased, and the peak of the benzene ring became a broader peak with weaker intensity, which consistently with the weakened of the carbon peak from Raman spectrum (Fig. 8c–e), indicating that part of the $c{-}0$ bond and benzene ring in the ligand dissociated after OER. The XPS of C 1s is in good agreement with observations in the FT-IR spectra. In the C 1s of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF after OER (Fig. S13b), the $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}0$ bond decreased, and two new peaks appeared at 292.38 and $295.14~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which are attributed to large $\pi$ -bonds [57]. This also confirms the partial dissociation of the ligand and the creation of large $\pi$ -bonds, which result in the formation of electron channels in the catalyst to facilitate the electron transport. Interestingly, we observed that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF after the OER formed many ‘‘green channels” of electrons on a macroscopic scale by SEM (Fig. S13c). After the dehydrogenation and deoxygenation steps of the OER, undercoordinated metal centers on the surface of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF were saturated with OH in the alkaline electrolyte. And due to the dispersion of metal sites and dissociation of carbon peaks, NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF generated less crystalline NiOOH after OER (Fig. S13d).
## 4. Conclusion
In the present study, the effect of $\pi$ -interaction on oxygen evolution reaction (OER) in NiFe-based metal–organic framework (MOFs) electrocatalysts was investigated using highly $\pi$ -electronconjugated NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NFs and non-conjugated NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NFs. On the one hand, the ligands with $\pi$ -interaction effectively regulate the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF in higher spin states and lower $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ orbital occupancy, resulting in highly active reaction sites with high-valent Ni ions; on the other hand, the $\pi$ - $\pi$ stacking channels in NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF accelerate the electron migration from the bulk phase to the catalyst surface. The pinteraction synergistically promotes the rapid generation of highly active reaction sites by optimizing the electronic structure of the reaction sites and accelerating electron transfer in NiFe-BPDC MOF/NF. This interpretation is consistent with our analysis obtained by in-situ Raman and density functional theory calculations that NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NF with 0.8559 eV $\pi$ -interaction energy generate $\gamma{-}\mathrm{Ni}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}00\mathrm{H}$ active species at $1.4~\mathrm{V}$ for only $60~s$ while NiFe-CHDA MOFs/NFs generate $\gamma$ -NiOOH and FeOOH active species at $1.5\mathrm{V}$ for $520s$ Furthermore, the Ni d-band center energy level of NiFe-BPDC MOFs/NFs shifts downward, which is favorable for the binding strength of intermediates and optimizes the rate-determining step of OER. In addition, NiFe-BPDC $\mathsf{M O F s}/$ NF rapidly completes the deprotonation and deoxygenation steps, and it requires only $288~\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential to reach $100\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ with $100\mathrm{{h}}$ of stability, which means promising industrial application. Altogether, the present study extends previous studies on the NiFe-based MOFs as OER electrocatalysts [11,21] in that it demonstrates that improving the OER performance in the NiFe-based MOFs requires consideration of the $\pi$ -interaction of the ligands, which depend critically on ligands properties and its effect on the electronic structure of the reaction sites and electron transfer rate.
